  "full_response": "The **CAP theorem**, also known as **Brewer's theorem**, is a fundamental principle in distributed systems that states it is impossible for a distributed data store to simultaneously provide all three of the following guarantees:\n\n1. **Consistency (C)** – Every read receives the most recent write or an error. All nodes see the same data at the same time.\n2. **Availability (A)** – Every request receives a (non-error) response, even if some nodes fail. The system remains operational.\n3. **Partition Tolerance (P)** – The system continues to operate despite network partitions (communication failures between nodes).\n\n### **Implications of the CAP Theorem**\nSince network partitions are inevitable in distributed systems, the CAP theorem forces designers to choose between **consistency** and **availability** when a partition occurs. This leads to three possible system designs:\n\n1. **CP (Consistency + Partition Tolerance)**\n   - Sacrifices availability to ensure consistency.\n   - If a partition occurs, the system may block or return errors until consistency is restored.\n   - Example: **MongoDB (in strong consistency mode), HBase, ZooKeeper, etcd.**\n\n2. **AP (Availability + Partition Tolerance)**\n   - Sacrifices consistency to remain available.\n   - Nodes may return stale data during a partition, but the system remains operational.\n   - Example: **Cassandra, DynamoDB, CouchDB, Riak.**\n\n3. **CA (Consistency + Availability)**\n   - Sacrifices partition tolerance (only works in a single-node or non-partitioned network).\n   - Not practical for large-scale distributed systems.\n   - Example: **Traditional relational databases (MySQL, PostgreSQL) in non-distributed setups.**\n\n### **Practical Trade-offs in Distributed Databases**\n- **Eventual Consistency (AP Systems):**\n  - AP systems often use **eventual consistency**, where updates propagate asynchronously, and conflicts are resolved later (e.g., via vector clocks or last-write-wins).\n  - Example: **Amazon Dynamo, Cassandra.**\n\n- **Strong Consistency (CP Systems):**\n  - CP systems enforce strict consistency but may become unavailable during partitions.\n  - Example: **Google Spanner (uses TrueTime for global consistency), etcd.**\n\n- **Tunable Consistency (Hybrid Approaches):**\n  - Some databases allow tuning consistency levels per query (e.g., **Cassandra’s QUORUM, ONE,",
